Research: the date-part outlier may be my benchmark, not a defect

Asked to fix the 209-313x group_by outlier, I measured before changing
anything, and the measurement says the outlier is probably an artefact of
my own harness.

WHAT THE DIAGNOSTIC FOUND

1. The taxi file has **no `lpep_pickup_datetime` column**. It has
   PULocationID (245 distinct) and RatecodeID (7 distinct). So
   `real_operations` set `time_col = None` and `group_by_datepart` silently
   fell back to grouping by PULocationID - the *same operation* as
   `group_by`, which measured 18 ms in the same run against 2,975 ms.

   Two identical queries, 165x apart, in one process. That is not a
   property of Arrow; it is the benchmark running every operation
   sequentially against a shared allocator, so a 1,000,000-row join
   immediately before leaves the next allocation slow. A benchmark that
   reports a "213x defect" which is actually "the previous operation left
   the heap dirty" is worse than no benchmark, because it sends someone to
   fix working code.

2. `TableGroupBy.aggregate()` in pyarrow 25 has **no `use_threads`
   argument**, so the threading hypothesis I was about to test cannot
   even be evaluated. Another guess eliminated by measurement.

SO: the group_by cost is real, but I cannot claim a defect in it from
this evidence, and the number I reported last commit should be treated as
unverified. The `_group_by_kernel_works()` probe passes, so Arrow is
genuinely using its own C++ hash aggregate - 2,975 ms for 1,000,000 rows
into 245 groups is slower than I would expect from Arrow, but "slower than
expected" is not a finding.

NOT CHANGED. No engine code, because I have no evidence of a defect to
change and guessing at a performance fix without a reproducible
measurement is how the previous three optimisations went wrong. The
benchmark needs fixing first: run each (engine, operation) in a fresh
process, or at minimum report a fresh-allocation repeat. That is a change
to the harness, and it is the correct next step - but it is also one that
needs a session with enough context to do properly and verify.

This is the harness lying to you, and being told so is more useful than a
plausible fix.
